  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 114 AM MDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nStorms will be slow to wind down Tuesday night but should taper\noff by sunrise Wednesday. The upper high will still be meandering\nover NM but looks to weaken to around 596dam by Wednesday. Plenty\nof moisture beneath this high will allow for scattered to numerous\nstorms over the high terrain. Storm motions will still be slow and\nPWATs will remain at or above 1 inch, so heavy rainfall will\nremain in play. More of the same in store for Thursday, but the\nupper high will begin to shift westward, centering over western NM\nby the afternoon. A boundary over northeast NM will be an\nadditional focus for storm activity and this boundary should push\nsouthward across the eastern plains Thursday night. Triple digit\nheat will continue across portions of central and eastern NM both\nWed and Thu.\n\nThe upper high will strengthen as it shifts northwestward on \nFriday to near 600dam. Temps will get a bump across western NM as \na result. Meanwhile, the boundary nudging up to the Central \nMountain Chain will be a focus for more thunderstorm activity once\nagain. Outflows will result in a gap wind in the Rio Grande \nValley, which will push low level moisture westward. As a result \nSaturday looks to be quite active areawide as well, though finally\nwith a little more storm motion. Drier air may filter in from the\nnortheast on Sunday, reducing thunderstorm coverage.",
